#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "27249c57-7f17-5e3e-9a21-0518320f5076" :question/math-academy-id "e-8192" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/73e2bd9a47c9d16f93d5240db3c4babc36f9fbe703ac9e0af449acaa7528f804.png)\n\nA triangular plate occupies the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/6e8362574defb66da2f3a81265f216d5e82fe6f01d9db6dc6fde3879b58d137a.png) enclosed between the lines ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/0b759759dc6e40e6a983b68c1b1ac821c304183d638e47347d3dc15bb6d43711.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/e5edcbd505cc63ee5461688fda2a101b0e6d529c2cb8185906f9ee564108415d.png) as shown above. The plate's mass density function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/8cd15d36128c5a26b2b25f57faa5cc41ecbe3e94af66e9c053a3da0a5425bac4.png) is given by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/d83addab9edd09882eb2af07d7ceb9facb3c4ca85179e3ad771581f00e3c932f.png) Find the total mass of the triangular plate." :question/worked-solution "The mass of the triangular plate occupying the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/3c51a5e31ce9f37895e4e38935b015ba8fd40373baa1c8735bd63f3592968af9.png) with mass density ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/ca272ba1efbdf1cd3d7c6c0d41a91233ce4a64659e5c96d040f9ff2da45b6ddf.png) is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/457c03e2f0a2295eaf404978491ea77c038507ba33bb1125a2a05952d0565af3.png)\n\nNotice that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/4c3142a8a4beae1e14a9ca2a611a7101d9a2a4cfe553109ae20a1d1b0dd78f64.png)\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/d2a923f8537e18206a972cc41916ae6f9ee26abc93734cd0a73c50ffc165e08a.png) treating ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/0ea8d498dfe5ba44cab2ca474bceae4ef3205271d683d0fdeedf7ffd68a22a43.png) as a constant:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/a9b1d2210f33a1242fccf6517fbc63783381f19cdc48daf78685900497b0829f.png)\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/73c3b5d4a287ad14dceb771dbd299c9effc9f3aff16c0f4b4051737ed99b36ae.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/3e75cffa3638e6260cbe5493a8277a484fe594d41e20ad55588da0b2dcf21fca.png)\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the plate is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/327bb4cc2df638a85e6bbe52eeda604b942f097b691c0c2125d579596dfe54a8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128963 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "d565cc1a-f78f-5712-8a03-df4d4e8f3e21" :question/math-academy-id "e-8193" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\, 0 \\le y \\le 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x.$" :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}A\n= \\int_{0}^2 \\left[ \\int_{0}^1 (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[4x-3{y}^{2}x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4(1)-3{y}^{2}(1)+(1)^{2})-(4(0)-3{y}^{2}(0)-(0)^{2})]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y & =[5y-{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(5(2)-(2)^{3})-(5(0)-(0)^{3}) \\\\  & =(10-8)-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128964 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "92b9b5ea-1153-580a-a03a-94338f30fe3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8195" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/f0db928874dba8d872cde46d1467686010f3484765b2d7e39807b5a32a131b09.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a copper plate that occupies the region $D$ shown above, and has charge density \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the region $D$ represents one-quarter of the disk of radius $3$ centered at \n$\\left(0,0\\right).$\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) =  \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\rho(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ \\rho(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the curve $x^2+y^2=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ r & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r]\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\frac{9}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =18[-1-0] \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the quarter-disk is $Q = - 18.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddf19ee8-e4a9-58be-84e3-6035959466ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351890} {:answer/id #uuid "38113ef0-8c70-5c7a-af7f-0c0965e79e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351891} {:answer/id #uuid "5f461ca6-0bc5-5010-884f-fc7855979560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351892} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2ba121-7f6a-510a-bc53-41cfebe55de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351893} {:answer/id #uuid "e8783cd7-ac91-5fab-9b41-88d5c1ae5bb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8783cd7-ac91-5fab-9b41-88d5c1ae5bb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351894} :answer-field/id #uuid "940140ba-78d4-56ab-8511-9d38235c80ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b6109e1-fa03-5ff2-889e-5d33063d5268" :question/math-academy-id "q-38232" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/2d6dc14c5296291bffa9a942ef9465b27477ee4c58550faba507703c2781a54b.png)\n\nA thin sheet of caramel occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $x=1,y=x$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the sheet is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ Find the total mass of the caramel sheet." :question/worked-solution "The mass of the caramel sheet occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. \n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the $x$-limits are $1\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/281ea7be1ef48c149bb4bcfd09aa41357caa8bb28adfc48285d298fb3cf89f3c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):1\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4})-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the ice sheet is $M=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af0dc441-0236-59a8-a8a1-c86f956c5cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127239} {:answer/id #uuid "dccd5c27-287a-576e-9a47-c0a8fd419eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127240} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1bb420-5802-559c-bdf0-4d3553e0d996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127241} {:answer/id #uuid "5066a334-b266-538e-8fca-44a2257cc4bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127242} {:answer/id #uuid "600f232c-cbaf-5c0c-af1c-b505c33f84fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "600f232c-cbaf-5c0c-af1c-b505c33f84fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127243} :answer-field/id #uuid "6baaa5a7-39ce-5f3a-b3a9-673aa04335e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "948a84fa-2589-57e2-8b27-de170c3c28f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-38248" :question/problem "A metal plate occupies the region $D={(x,y):-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$ The charge density of the plate is given by $\\rho (x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate." :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/1cbe96aa4bf517302a2329db9f8eb2e9c56873e4d5a18cd5f1cd676543e97086.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{7}}{7}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{1}^{7}}{7}-(-\\frac{{1}^{7}}{7})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{1}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6dc0b97-5075-553e-b8d4-e36f538457c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351897} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ceeeb1-3c48-5548-926b-59b34347fa51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351898} {:answer/id #uuid "05322158-3cce-5a59-a6a5-6b62e24c96d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351899} {:answer/id #uuid "b010278b-ffdc-51bc-8384-2b154ac8dc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351900} {:answer/id #uuid "475c58c6-dbef-51a4-9119-09c6db559222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "475c58c6-dbef-51a4-9119-09c6db559222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351901} :answer-field/id #uuid "4be0b0c3-55af-5edf-8f4e-9598fd761675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "79fcdab8-3030-59a8-9b3c-1d84b6ba1d6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38942" :question/problem "Find the mass of a glass object which has the form of an upper half-disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The mass of an upper half-disk $D$ of radius $3$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/d8e2f73ce8840471143689748e188debaf5fde8f3aaf5021fd8d99c4f86a72d6.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}(-(-1)+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the upper half-disk is $M=\\frac{81}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b12f7cf6-6124-546d-bff9-8fae07230cb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351904} {:answer/id #uuid "712689ff-6489-58aa-a834-7fc7e0e91cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351905} {:answer/id #uuid "8b09075e-13b4-54ff-94fa-6565a2a6f034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351906} {:answer/id #uuid "43ded51e-9d16-55fe-a5ed-26e1d86f956c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351907} {:answer/id #uuid "d936791a-4508-5918-9866-9747673d4277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "712689ff-6489-58aa-a834-7fc7e0e91cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351905} :answer-field/id #uuid "19f77149-1c1c-5e30-9dc6-dce6454adaa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f0d81d9-3cb8-57b4-8dc6-522b22cded6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94170" :question/problem "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular wooden flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=6{x}^{2}y.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=6{x}^{2}y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6y[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6y{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6y(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}32y\\,\\text{d}y & =[16{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot ({1}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12212810-b7fa-5a0a-b7c7-9206c8e11cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351911} {:answer/id #uuid "c7996723-6436-563a-8cd6-44f034464fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351912} {:answer/id #uuid "85d322b0-9eb7-552f-a546-eb999000fefc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351913} {:answer/id #uuid "e3550474-f463-5e06-9565-13b16f0939fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351914} {:answer/id #uuid "874caefe-59e0-57b3-bf0e-60be4f171828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3550474-f463-5e06-9565-13b16f0939fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351914} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5d4505-d90b-5e8a-acad-b3fb5fd5e63b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abc8b0fb-8221-56d4-b127-e2438d953bfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94174" :question/problem "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular iron flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{2}\\le x\\le {e}^{6},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{2}\\le x\\le {e}^{6},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}[{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{6}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y & =[{y}^{4}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={3}^{4}-(0)^{4} \\\\  & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M=81.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "888c450f-0b77-5a0b-887c-6d58590b4a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351918} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0cae52-6494-5d86-9108-22e5163f8778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351919} {:answer/id #uuid "01abbb5b-dc08-5ed1-87d8-5e9a0fc3dd7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351920} {:answer/id #uuid "944e6028-c801-5bc0-b3ed-dad5cdfb3ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351921} {:answer/id #uuid "525d7a3d-43db-5cb6-8a50-4fc5ca4c1da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01abbb5b-dc08-5ed1-87d8-5e9a0fc3dd7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351920} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0fb7e99-43ea-541b-bfe1-8623853d03f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff5e5f5f-7934-515a-b5b1-052ee590a017" :question/math-academy-id "q-94221" :question/problem "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M=\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09dc945c-4250-5e07-9fe0-00a9c144f9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351925} {:answer/id #uuid "f1176113-6d0e-52b9-b666-c6a47a666ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351926} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb2c1c6-8db1-5ad6-b1eb-839fc383afe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351927} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7f650b-e53b-5bc6-9c48-27c6a0d6a5b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351928} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff905e1-2522-51f0-9dc9-fd577797a5be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09dc945c-4250-5e07-9fe0-00a9c144f9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351925} :answer-field/id #uuid "126335fd-eb30-522a-b418-fe0a7ca93cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b393f80-2259-5a57-ae72-0a254617a38c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94224" :question/problem "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=y{e}^{xy}.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{e}^{xy}]_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y & =[{e}^{y}-y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-2)-(1-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M={e}^{2}-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6ae9be1-ec4c-58bd-aa08-b389e49fdd51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351932} {:answer/id #uuid "038a80b2-235f-59e9-850f-46eff9dc3150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351933} {:answer/id #uuid "78d9e1a7-2ff0-5a17-98e0-a64704f1cad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351934} {:answer/id #uuid "a46b1c88-b41f-54e7-95a3-5394f3380f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351935} {:answer/id #uuid "7267e4d9-da8b-5530-a7cd-32ba51c56950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6ae9be1-ec4c-58bd-aa08-b389e49fdd51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351932} :answer-field/id #uuid "0226aa79-f71f-5837-b61b-219722b3a652" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75df3b07-0877-5a7c-b6c0-1325b20ac75b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94389" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/c497efbb28bade962b997d7e14f5a272f0cead56f7567b1baaebae695c7c80f4.png)\n\nAn iron flat plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $x=0,y=e,$ and $y={e}^{x},$ as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}.$ Find the total mass of the plate." :question/worked-solution "The mass of the plate occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. \n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & =e \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the $x$-limits are $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/95a6d9b0bc464c719311dc900967ebd06a3aa3d3004cfe5303359eecb20551e7.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{e}^{x}\\le y\\le e}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the iron plate is $M=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd41dfb6-e8c8-53f5-93ae-55acbcf6e65e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351939} {:answer/id #uuid "3af58a3f-bdf4-5c33-81a5-81bd139eb32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351940} {:answer/id #uuid "9287031f-dd1d-5e59-bf58-59bd63a53c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351941} {:answer/id #uuid "469a2a80-60d2-5811-b829-61b338fb8b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351942} {:answer/id #uuid "e95fd766-2362-5e60-b4cc-d626d7dc1c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9287031f-dd1d-5e59-bf58-59bd63a53c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351941} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9346e13-a39b-5300-9db8-743f5b792ca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13b347ac-ad08-5860-805b-00e480d8cc18" :question/math-academy-id "q-94437" :question/problem "A metal plate occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\pi },\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}.$ The charge density of the plate is given by $\\rho (x,y)=x.$ Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate." :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/2b179e35ae9af5a13d43c9a64bee99ee4ed87c257b623184f9165e14c24f3dee.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}[y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the plate is $Q=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba9d50a0-d3d6-5b98-ad38-1986d41cce4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351946} {:answer/id #uuid "0adc9b6b-20a9-55b1-83fd-6525cdef1ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351947} {:answer/id #uuid "cc28393e-8be9-5583-94ea-d56ce1d3832d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351948} {:answer/id #uuid "e85eee60-8b00-55ee-bae8-9cbdd9472101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351949} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1a399f-349c-524b-9246-05559e69bf1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e85eee60-8b00-55ee-bae8-9cbdd9472101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351949} :answer-field/id #uuid "d87f2432-7b26-59c3-b7f3-f64ea0421e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44761544-bc59-5004-b679-18f03588f956" :question/math-academy-id "q-94535" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{y}]}_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85cd58d8-459a-515a-b0d9-664fa8fad965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351953} {:answer/id #uuid "d827604d-a55d-560c-9cce-8e34787a78c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351954} {:answer/id #uuid "5f876516-3967-5257-a953-1f391002b77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351955} {:answer/id #uuid "e38925d6-a3f3-5a68-81cb-ab3b04459851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351956} {:answer/id #uuid "7a311b3d-ef6d-51a1-96bd-5af786b36880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e38925d6-a3f3-5a68-81cb-ab3b04459851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351956} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9362177-3c5b-5914-8f73-9e34645a2ecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "550b89e0-e302-5d13-a797-fa0b28af8134" :question/math-academy-id "q-94586" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/7583a2da0bff7d66a3512c58f67692100af403b61b19a9b52347519b30823342.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed between  the parts of  the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ as shown above, and has charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the region $D$ represents a half-annulus enclosed between the circles of the radiuses $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\rho (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\rho (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}[r]_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\, \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the object is $Q=\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64162a4a-2ea8-52f2-9bce-a2e2151cef7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351960} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2c2c0f-8207-566e-87eb-8bab87ac022d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351961} {:answer/id #uuid "702ad8c3-97ef-5409-a7f3-16acf1042020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351962} {:answer/id #uuid "ff390992-506e-50aa-a187-66cf85849127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351963} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ab9c97-323f-5fe7-acc7-17b5c8a6189c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "702ad8c3-97ef-5409-a7f3-16acf1042020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351962} :answer-field/id #uuid "8afd0c82-dffe-59d7-93fa-7bea7d2bd4fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49846630-f9bc-5f81-85c3-35e7a92041fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94720" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/514a3cd39f24a7b2477e6214964d69cd743a26645eba7f4b797af832b565fa93.png)\n\nAn object occupies the region $D$ in the second quadrant enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$, as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the object is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=y.$ Find the mass of the object." :question/worked-solution "The mass of an object which occupies the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will convert this to polar coordinates using the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nThe following equations connect cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/7f3b2717946956ade07c252413130a730070e370cab85c73d866fa9506604106.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (9-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the object is $M=\\frac{26}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d22b61a-6ae2-5394-9a9c-39e6248b2a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351967} {:answer/id #uuid "a40d46aa-aded-5e1c-94b7-93b3cdcfc88b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351968} {:answer/id #uuid "a9006812-238d-533e-a48b-3c43af1e8082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351969} {:answer/id #uuid "971cab19-5ab9-5e25-8f85-58301c5759be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351970} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b4eb53-1470-5051-884b-ce03a3a4f9e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d22b61a-6ae2-5394-9a9c-39e6248b2a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351967} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae14ce4-0f42-5d18-978b-a9d596a1b573" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcf1f489-2f73-5a5e-978f-c808e77f0ba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94750" :question/problem "Find the mass of a plastic object which has the form of a lower half-disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The mass of a lower half-disk $D$ of radius $4$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/379073190f15a5e80c696b8f5bb77132dd515ca2493b8f0407354bbac923bb91.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (64-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32[\\theta -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2}]_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the upper half-disk is $M=32\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1676f25-de24-595f-9009-beefae456357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351974} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4d151f-a4c4-5144-aa5a-7f77a6305f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351975} {:answer/id #uuid "15443454-96c2-5b24-9f52-1f2b4db0c8af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351976} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e93153-f54d-5207-ac15-07aad4c707d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "162\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351977} {:answer/id #uuid "38b5e474-ec12-59b0-8e1b-328d44c82073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c4d151f-a4c4-5144-aa5a-7f77a6305f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351975} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa6e0e0-eaa7-5640-94fe-7f85275a03f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c162a69-51f7-54a5-bf8b-83b3d93dcc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-94753" :question/problem "Find the mass of an ice object which has the form of a quarter-disk, in the fourth quadrant, of radius $3$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "The mass of a quarter-disk $D$ of radius $3$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/4f7b1647d893512605ee1a2e9cee3e5511a00dc7ad9b1765fff1d0ba0710895f.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{3}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(81-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =162(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the quarter-disk is $M=81\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa696aaf-2501-5152-af0f-e7dd8e1b6ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351981} {:answer/id #uuid "3a38556d-8907-5065-bb62-49aa16f7d0ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351982} {:answer/id #uuid "3252c881-dc00-5001-a4f8-46f9501dff45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351983} {:answer/id #uuid "127976cc-f92d-5c8e-9845-5b0163b4c696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351984} {:answer/id #uuid "36a902e7-7d87-5010-ad39-5f2be1fd74fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "127976cc-f92d-5c8e-9845-5b0163b4c696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351984} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9ae5c24-6780-53b4-9872-8031a2cfa2a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5bf2603-5b6f-5151-a667-e918275bd950" :question/math-academy-id "q-95141" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of the lower half of the circular disk ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9$ with charge density function $\\rho $ given by $\\rho (x,y)=y$" :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of  the lower half of the circular disk  $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,y\\le 0}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince $y\\le 0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\,⟹\\,y\\ge -\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/af77552bd144fb70dfe8787060c0136f54955d20cdd2543391bd040b01a460d2.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the circle with the line $y=0.$ So, the $x$-limits are $-3\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/32db7009737aa47c8b5a96da91d72b0db95530271f463a9f7d35f715793cbea8.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le 3,\\,-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}^{0}y\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}^{0}y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}^{0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}(0-\\frac{9-{x}^{2}}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({x}^{2}-9)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({x}^{2}-9)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-9x]}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(9-27)-(-9+27)] \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the lower half of the circular disk is $Q=-18.$"}]}
